<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Papua</h1><p class="st-meta">1906 CE – 1949 CE · capital Port Moresby</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Australia's first colonial territory, formerly British New Guinea, administered for over three decades by Sir Hubert Murray.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> transferred <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-new-guinea">British New Guinea</a> to the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-act-1905">Papua Act</a> of 1905, in force from 1906, renamed it the Territory of Papua, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>. It was the first overseas territory governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, and it took over the government stations and village constable system built up under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-macgregor">William MacGregor</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-murray">Hubert Murray</a>, lieutenant governor from 1908 until his death in 1940, followed a paternalistic policy that restricted the sale of land to Europeans, relied on armed constabulary and village constables, and made punitive raids rarer. The European population remained small, and plantations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold mining developed only modestly. Missions provided most schooling and health care. Papuans had no political rights, and the economy was poorer than that of the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-new-guinea">Territory of New Guinea</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Japanese advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a> was stopped on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokoda-track-campaign">Kokoda Track</a> in 1942 with help from Papuan carriers. Civil rule was suspended and both territories were run by a single military administration. In 1949 Papua was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea">Territory of Papua and New Guinea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-papua.jpg" alt="Territory of Papua" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Territory_of_Papua.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1906 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Moresby</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hubert Murray</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous religions, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Hiri Motu, Papuan and Austronesian languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Papua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=territory-of-papua" aria-label="Territory of Papua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8741 2531 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Papua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9061 2787L9061 2787M9061 2787L8774 3709M9061 2787L9061 2787"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9061 2787h0M8774 3709h0M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9061 2787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Moresby. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=territory-of-papua">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
